import { TerminalCopy } from "../model"

pub fn copy -> TerminalCopy = TerminalCopy {
  summary: "Process is the service type required to read the running program’s settings, arguments, and working directory.",
  taskTitle: "Declare what a setting reader needs to run",
  purpose: "Read the startup setting SESERAGI_DOCS_MODE and display the result. Here, focus on what process: process.Process requires in the operation’s declaration.",
  typescriptBefore: "In Node.js or Bun TypeScript, process from node:process provides access to environment variables. This example chooses a default when the process.env value is undefined and preserves every present value, including an empty string.",
  benefit: "Putting Process in a Seseragi with declaration records that the operation needs information from its execution environment. It does not store a setting inside a Process value; it declares the need for a service that can read settings.",
  reading: [
    "In with Console, process: process.Process, process: names the required service. The process.Process to its right names the type from std/process, imported under the alias process. Neither is a setting name or setting value.",
    "Console in the same with declaration is the service used by println. Reading process information and writing output are separate roles, so this main declares both.",
    "Even a successful process.environment read returns Nothing if the setting is absent, or Just mode if it is present. Process requires an environment capable of the read; it does not promise that a particular setting exists."
  ],
  details: [
    "You normally do not construct the internals of Process yourself. The process-target execution environment supplies the services main requires. An import makes type and operation names available; importing alone does not read a setting.",
    "This example handles the read’s ProcessError locally and chooses a message. The subsequent println can still fail separately, which is why the main declaration retains fails ConsoleError.",
    "arguments and currentDirectory require the same Process service. This is not a type for reading settings or arguments from a browser: the example uses the process target, launched with Node.js or Bun."
  ]
